{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Landed: Gerrit change 3127 merged (3835269d792) with the snapshot-close-metadata ensure unit. Live repair run with `rebar store fsck --repair` on 2026-10-28, 14:00:22 to 14:33:03 PDT (32 min 41 s wall). Ensure sweep: rebuilt all 12 stale SNAPSHOTs (075b, 14f4, 40a7, 52b0, 7332, 76b2, 84e6, 858c, a881, a8ea, ad81, e215), 30 to 74 s each, about 10.5 min in total; `ensures: 10/10 applied`. The a3 remediation committed batch 1 (313 files) and then aborted because its push was rejected as non-fast-forward (origin had moved); the remaining batches did not run, and fsck issues went from 102 to 30. Re-run `rebar store fsck --repair` to finish the remediation. Earlier measurements in this ticket came from a dry run through a cp -R copy of .tickets-tracker; that copy's .git file points at the live gitdir, so those 12 rebuilds, and a `git remote remove` run through the copy, hit the live tracker repo (local rebuild commits were rolled back to origin before this repair). Boot-time sweeps (op-cert workspace, MCP boot) also run this unit; on an unconverged origin each costs the rebuild time, and on a converged one about 21.5 s per sweep."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793223294460109564,"uuid":"0922b541-d57e-462a-b760-ecb934979788"}